steps.body:
`class Child extends Parent` makes a class that starts with everything the parent has. Use it when one kind of thing is a more specific version of another: a dog is an animal.

```javascript harness
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  describe() {
    return `${this.name} is an animal.`;
  }
}

{{code}}

console.log(new Dog("Rex", "collie").describe());
```

```javascript
class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name, breed) {
    super(name);        // runs Animal's constructor
    this.breed = breed; // then add the extra field
  }

  describe() {
    return `${super.describe()} Breed: ${this.breed}`;
  }
}
```

- `super(...)` calls the parent constructor, so the parent sets up its own fields such as `this.name`.
- In a child constructor, `super(...)` must run before you touch `this`. Using `this` first, or never calling `super(...)` at all, throws a ReferenceError.
- A child with no constructor gets one for free that passes every argument to the parent.
- Instances inherit the parent's fields and methods, so `new Dog("Rex", "collie").name` works.
- Inside a method, `super.describe()` calls the parent's version. Wrap it to extend the parent instead of copying its code.

When input lines start with a kind word, read that word first and build the matching class.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const lines = input.trim().split("\n");

class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  describe() {
    return `${this.name} is an animal.`;
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name, breed) {
    super(name);
    this.breed = breed;
  }

  describe() {
    return `${super.describe()} Breed: ${this.breed}`;
  }
}

for (const line of lines) {
  const [kind, name, breed] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  const pet = kind === "dog" ? new Dog(name, breed) : new Animal(name);
  console.log(pet.describe());
}

