steps.body:
A subclass **overrides** a parent method by defining one with the same name. JavaScript finds the child's method before the parent's, so the child's version wins.

That gives you **polymorphism**: one loop calls `shape.area()` and each object runs its own version. The loop never asks which class it holds.

```javascript harness
{{code}}

class Circle extends Shape {
  constructor(radius) {
    super();
    this.name = "circle";
    this.radius = radius;
  }

  area() {
    return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2;
  }
}

console.log(new Circle(1).describe());
```

```javascript
class Shape {
  area() {
    throw new Error("not implemented"); // every subclass must override
  }

  describe() {
    return `${this.name}: ${this.area().toFixed(2)}`; // calls the child's area()
  }
}
```

A base method that throws makes a forgotten override fail loudly instead of quietly returning `undefined`.

`x instanceof Shape` checks the whole chain: a `Circle` is an instance of `Circle`, of `Shape`, and of `Object`.

