steps.body:
Errors are classes too, so you can extend `Error` to make error types for your own program.

```javascript
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(field, message) {
    super(message);                // sets this.message
    this.name = "ValidationError"; // otherwise name stays "Error"
    this.field = field;            // extra data for whoever catches it
  }
}
console.log(String(new ValidationError("age", "too old"))); // ValidationError: too old
```

- `super(message)` lets `Error` set `message`. Set `this.name` yourself, or it prints as plain `Error`.
- Extra fields such as `field` or `amount` let the catch block react without parsing the message text.

Catch by type with `instanceof`. A `ValidationError` is also an `instanceof Error`, so test the most specific class. Handle the errors you expect and rethrow everything else, so a real bug such as a TypeError is not silently swallowed. This is the rethrow from Error Handling written as a guard clause. Keep the parentheses: `!error instanceof ValidationError` applies `!` to `error` first, so it is always false.

```javascript harness
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(field, message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ValidationError";
    this.field = field;
  }
}

try {
  throw new ValidationError("age", "must be 0-150");
{{code}}
```

